    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 tracking-tight mb-3">
      Septic Distribution Box Leveling &amp; Flow Equalization Rules
    </h1>
    <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-6">
      A residential septic drainfield is designed as a balanced parallel network: each perforated absorption trench is calculated to absorb a specific daily gallon volume based on soil percolation rates. If the distribution box (D-box) is out of level by even <strong>1/16 of an inch</strong>, gravity forces the vast majority of liquid effluent into the lowest outlet port. Here is the geotechnical and plumbing guide to leveling, sub-base preparation, and installing <strong>eccentric flow-equalizer speed levelers</strong>.
    </p>

    <!-- Key Metrics Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 p-4 bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 mb-6">
      <div>
        <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 font-medium">Leveling Tolerance</div>
        <div class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">&plusmn; 1/16 Inch</div>
        <div class="text-xs text-blue-600">Across All Outlet Inverts</div>
      </div>
      <div>
