    expertName="David Miller, PE"
    role="Senior Structural Engineer & Soil Mechanics Specialist"
    experience="24+ Years Field Experience"
    avatarBg="bg-blue-100 text-blue-800"
    initials="DM"
    title="Why Hydrogen Sulfide Gas Dissolves Concrete Distribution Boxes from the Top Down"
    note="During septic system forensic inspections, I routinely pry the lid off a 15-year-old precast concrete D-box only to watch the concrete crumble like wet chalk in my hands. The bottom invert may look fine, but the upper walls and lid are completely eaten away. This is caused by biogenic sulfuric acid corrosion. Anaerobic bacteria in the septic tank generate hydrogen sulfide gas (H2S), which smells like rotten eggs. In the presence of moisture and air in the D-box headspace, aerobic Thiobacillus bacteria colonize the damp concrete ceiling and convert H2S gas into pure sulfuric acid (H2SO4). The sulfuric acid chemically attacks the alkaline calcium silicate hydrate in Portland cement, transforming hard concrete into mushy gypsum paste (calcium sulfate). If you are replacing an old concrete D-box, do not install another concrete box. Upgrade to an engineered High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE) polymer box (such as Tuf-Tite or Polylok). High-density plastic is 100% immune to acid corrosion and will last indefinitely underground."
  />

